subcode:: sections
    $call section, syntax, Basic $(code:MyDef) syntax
    $p
        Before diving into details, here is a quick reference of the core concepts in $(code:MyDef):
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Concept"
            $th "Definition"
            $th "Usage"
            $th "Scope"
        $tr
            $td "Page"
            $td
                $(code:page: name)
            $td "compiled automatically into an output file"
            $td "file"
        $tr
            $td "Subcode"
            $td
                $(code:subcode: name(params))
            $td
                $(code:$call name, args)
            $td "global or page"
        $tr
            $td "Block call"
            $td
                subcode with $(code:BLOCK) keyword
            $td
                $(code:&call name, args) + indented block
            $td "nested"
        $tr
            $td "Macro"
            $td
                $(code:macros:) block
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(name))
            $td "global or page"
        $tr
            $td "Dynamic macro"
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(set:name=value))
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(name))
            $td "current scope"
        $tr
            $td "fncode"
            $td
                $(code:fncode: name(params))
            $td "target language function call"
            $td "global"

    $call subsection, syntax_defile, Basic structure of $(code:.def) file
    $p
        The basic structure of $(code:.def) file is indentation based. An example $(code:.def) file may look like this:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            include: common.def
            include: macros/utils.def

            &num; This is a comment
            page: test
                Line 1
                Line 2
                $call A
                lines after

            subcode: A
                line a - $(msg_a)
                line b - $(msg_b)

            macros:
                msg_a: this is message a
                msg_b: this is message b
    $p
        Compiling this with $(code:mydef_page) produces a file $(code:test.txt) containing:
        &call codeprint
            Line 1
            Line 2
            line a - this is message a
            line b - this is message b
            lines after
    $p
        The $(code:$call A) was replaced by the body of $(code:subcode: A), and the macros $(code:&dollar;(msg_a)) and $(code:&dollar;(msg_b)) were expanded to their values. This is the essence of $(code:MyDef): subcodes are inlined and macros are expanded, producing the final output.
    $p
        At the top indentation level, only headings $(code:include:), $(code:page:), $(code:subcode:), and $(code:macros:) are recognized by $(code:parseutil.pm). However, any additional lines not under these headings do not cause $(code:MyDef) errors. They will be silently ignored. So a Knuth-style literate programming can be achieved with $(code:MyDef) by freely mixing documentation (at the top indentation level or under any unrecognized headings) and code (under these recognized headings).
    $p
        Explicit comments can be introduced with $(code:&num;) at any indentation level or between code or at the end of any lines.
    $p
        $(code:include:) adds additional files to a list and they will be loaded in the order of addition. Note that this is different from the C preprocessor. A file can be included multiple times but will only be loaded once. The included file will be searched for in the current working directory, $(code:include_path) settings in the $(code:config) file, and the environment variable $(code:$MYDEFLIB).

    $p
        $(code:page:) defines the output file name and its main code. Each line in the main code will be copied, macro-expanded, or translated, and finally dumped to the output file. For output modules other than $(code:output_general), there may be additional lines inserted or appended to the main code. For example, $(code:output_perl) will automatically insert $(code:#!) lines and $(code:use strict;) for Perl scripts.
    $p
        $(code:subcode:) defines blocks of code that can be inserted into main code or another subcode by syntax like $(code:$call codename). The syntax resembles function definitions and function calls in a programming language. However, it is more appropriate to think of subcodes as merely multi-line macros that are expanded at the block or line level. Their usage is very similar to inline macros and they will be expanded regardless of the underlying language syntax. $(code:subcodes) allow reorganization of code according to higher logic than the semantics of a programming language.
    $p
        $(code:macros:) defines a set of name-value definitions. They are used as inline macros. To use these macros and have them expanded in the output, one can use syntax like $(code:&dollar;(msg_a)). This syntax was chosen to avoid collision with common language syntax. Having macros stand out makes the subtle differences between macros and certain programming language entities such as variables and functions explicit. This is in fact helpful for both writing and reading programs.

    $p
        The code block under $(code:page:) is actually a $(code:subcode) with name $(code:main). In fact, it is the same to declare main code like this:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            page: test
                subcode: main
                    line 1
                    line 2
                    ...
    $p
        Upon compilation, each $(code:page) output looks for a $(code:main) subcode. If not found, the output will be blank regardless of how many $(code:subcodes) or $(code:macros) are defined in the $(code:def) files.
    $p
        Not all $(code:def) files need to contain $(code:page:) directives. In practice, most $(code:def) files contain only $(code:macro) and $(code:subcode) definitions and are included via $(code:include:) into the main $(code:def) file.
    $p
        The overall structure of $(code:def) files is declarative. The order of $(code:include:), $(code:page:), $(code:subcode:), and $(code:macros:) does not matter -- in most cases. The few cases where order does matter will be discussed in the following chapters.
    $p
        Indentation needs to be strictly observed directly under $(code:page:), $(code:subcode:), and $(code:macros:). More specifically, all lines that are supposed to be under the directive need to have indentation at or deeper than the first line's indentation. Otherwise, part of the code block or definitions may be ignored during parsing. The code under $(code:subcode), which includes the main code under $(code:page), may contain additional indentation within. All this internal indentation will be preserved and passed to output. However, during parsing, the leading spaces of a line are stripped and encoded as just the indentation level. Upon output, the indentation is added back by inserting 4 spaces for each indentation level. So the output code may look slightly different from what is defined in $(code:subcode). For most languages, leading spaces are insignificant and only the indentation level matters, so $(code:MyDef)'s indentation treatment should not pose any problem. In fact, it helps make the output code more readable.

subcode::sections
    $call subsection, syntax_scope, Scope
    $p
        Both $(code:subcode) and $(code:macros) can be nested and they follow the visibility of the scope. It is possible to structure code like this:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            macros:
                A: global macro

            subcode: A
                global subcode. 
                Inline macros and subcodes have different namespace.

            page: test
                m: page-level macros can be directly defined here, but only directly under page: directive
                &num; page defines a page scope

                macros:
                    p: this is also a page scope macros

                subcode: main
                    These subcodes (include main) are defined at the page scope
                    $call A &num; works, global scope is visible
                    $call a &num; works, page scope is visible
                    $call c &num; won't work, issues error message

                subcode: a
                    This is at page scope

                    subcode: c
                        nested subcode

                    macros:
                        q: only visible inside subcode: a

    $p
        Scopes are observed during expansion time. In the above example, normally $(code:subcode: a) inside $(code:page) is not visible to $(code:subcode:A). However, when A is called inside the $(code:page), the scope of $(code:subcode:A) becomes nested under the $(code:page) scope, and $(code:subcode: a) becomes accessible. While this expansion-time scoping rule is flexible and can be used to enhance code readability in certain scenarios, it is also very easy to abuse and create spaghetti code. Apply common sense.

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, dynamic_macro, Dynamic inline macros
    $p
        Using the $(code:macros:) directive is not the only way to define inline macros. Because the $(code:macros:) directive has to be used away from where the macros are being used, it is only useful for macros that need to be visible across larger scopes. For local macro usage, there is another syntax to define them:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: A
                $(set:a=A_very_long_array_variable[i])
                $(a) = $(a) * $(a)

    $p
        At global context, long and descriptive names are desirable; but at small local context, short names are desirable. Macros provide simple and straight solutions. It is nice to be able to define it right where it is being used so programmers don't have to scroll far to infer the meaning. The local macro automatically expires at the end of block context, which saves the worry of name pollution.

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, subcode_param, Subcode with parameters
    $p
        There are two ways to pass parameters into a $(code:subcode). We can always $(code:set) macros right before we $(code:&dollar;call) the $(code:subcode) as mentioned in the previous section. The drawback of this method is that the parameter signature is not defined where the $(code:subcode) is defined. This can be remedied by adding comments, but is nevertheless less than ideal. Also, the calling interface is somewhat clumsy.
    $p
        We can directly define $(code:subcode) with following syntax:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: A(greet, name)
                printf("$(greet), $(name)!\n");

            page: t
                $call A, Hello, World
                $call A, Howdy, Guest

    $p
        Multiple parameter names can be defined in the $(code:subcode:) line inside parentheses, separated by commas. Correspondingly, $(code:&dollar;call) needs to append actual values for the parameters after the $(code:subcode) name, separated by commas. In implementation, parameters are essentially the same as inline macros and are used equivalently.
    $p
        The number of values passed in at $(code:&dollar;call) time must match the number of parameters defined in the $(code:subcode). A mismatch will raise errors. The values at $(code:&dollar;call) are split simply by comma (and any spaces that follow). This can pose a problem when the value itself contains a comma. In practice, there are a few heuristic implementation rules that make these situations tolerable. For one, if the comma is enclosed in parentheses or quotation marks, it will not be treated as a separator:
        &call codeprint mydef
            subcode: A(greeting)
                printf($(greeting));

            page: t
                $call A, "Hello, World!\n"

    $p
        Of course this will not automatically solve all use cases. In fact, in some cases you may want the comma to act as a separator despite quotation marks or parentheses around it. For tricky situations, $(code:MyDef) may choose simply not to provide support. Supporting tricky situations often complicates both code and usage, and sometimes explicitly not supporting them may be the better option. You can always avoid tricky feature usage by not using the feature at all and writing your code directly, letting $(code:MyDef) pass it through to output.
    $p
        That said, in the case that you want the usage to absorb all remaining text as a single parameter, there is a syntax for that:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: A(greet, @names)
                $print $(greet), $(names)!

            page: t
                module: perl
                $call A, Hello, Alice, Bob, and Carol
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_run t.def
            PAGE: t
              --> [./t.pl]
            perl ./t.pl
            Hello, Alice, Bob, and Carol!
    $p
        Prepending $(code:@) to the last parameter name in the $(code:subcode:) definition signals the $(code:MyDef) compiler to absorb all remaining text at $(code:&dollar;call) time. Obviously, $(code:@param) will only work for the last (or only) parameter. In addition, $(code:@param) also signals the $(code:MyDef) compiler that it is OK to have empty values for that parameter, so $(code:MyDef) will not raise an error even when the value is missing at $(code:&dollar;call) time. Use it with caution.

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, block_call, Block call
    $p
        One of the main purposes of $(code:subcode) is to capture text patterns. A common pattern may consist of an opening part and a closing part. In many languages and other implementations, this kind of pattern requires two or more separate subcode definitions, called separately as well. It is ugly. For this type of pattern, $(code:MyDef) supports the $(code:&call) syntax:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: tag(name)
                <$(name)>
                    BLOCK
                </$(name)>

            page: t
                &call tag, html
                    &call tag, body
                        &call tag, h1
                            Hello, World!

    $p
        Note that $(code:BLOCK) is indented one level inside the opening and closing tags. This indentation is preserved in the output, so nested block calls produce properly indented results:
        &call codeprint
            <html>
                <body>
                    <h1>
                        Hello, World!
                    </h1>
                </body>
            </html>
    $p
        If $(code:BLOCK) were at the same indentation level as the tags, the output would be flat with no nesting indentation.
    $p
        $(code:&call) requires an indented block following the call, where code is expected to be inserted inside the subcode expansion, replacing the keyword $(code:BLOCK).
        $(code:BLOCK) is one of a few special $(code:MyDef) keywords in addition to the $(code:&dollar;(macro...)) syntax.
    $p
        The code that replaces $(code:BLOCK) will have its own scope and is nested inside the $(code:subcode) being called, and therefore can access all macros and subcodes that are defined within. For example, you may use block call purely for context:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: Parsing(line)
                BLOCK

                subcode: parse_string
                    $if $(line)=~/"([^"]*)"/
                        BLOCK
                subcode: parse_number
                    ...

            page: t
                module: perl
                my $text = 'say "hi"'
                &call Parsing, $text
                    &call parse_string
                        $print got string $1
    $p
        $(code:parse_string) is visible only inside the $(code:Parsing) block. The output is:
        &call codeprint, perl
            my $text = 'say "hi"';
            if ($text=~/"([^"]*)"/) {
                print "got string $1\n";
            }

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, call_vs_blockcall, $(code:$call) vs $(code:&call)
    $p
        To summarize the difference between the two calling conventions:
    $ul
        $li
            $(code:$call name, args) -- inline expansion. The subcode body is inserted directly. No indented block follows.
        $li
            $(code:&call name, args) -- block expansion. An indented block must follow. That block replaces the $(code:BLOCK) keyword inside the subcode body.
    $p
        Both use the same subcode definition. A subcode with $(code:BLOCK) can be invoked either way: $(code:$call) skips the $(code:BLOCK) placeholder, while $(code:&call) fills it. Note that using $(code:$call) on a subcode containing $(code:BLOCK) will produce a warning: $(code:BLOCK called out of context!). The warning is harmless -- the $(code:BLOCK) line is simply omitted from the output. Consider:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: section(title)
                --- $(title) ---
                BLOCK
                --- end $(title) ---

            page: t
                &num; inline call -- BLOCK is skipped:
                $call section, Empty

                &num; block call -- BLOCK is filled:
                &call section, Content
                    Line 1
                    Line 2
    $p
        The output would be:
        &call codeprint
            --- Empty ---
            --- end Empty ---
            --- Content ---
            Line 1
            Line 2
            --- end Content ---

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, map_call, Map call
    $p
        For subcodes with a single parameter, sometimes it is desirable to call them multiple times with various parameters concisely. For this, we can use the $(code:&dollar;map) call:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: greet(name)
                push @guests, "$(name)"
                $print Hello $(name)!

            page: t
                module: perl
                $map greet, Alice, Bob, Carol

    $p
        The subcode is called once for each parameter. The output $(code:t.pl) would be:
        &call codeprint, perl
            &num;!/usr/bin/perl
            use strict;

            push @guests, "Alice";
            print "Hello Alice!\n";
            push @guests, "Bob";
            print "Hello Bob!\n";
            push @guests, "Carol";
            print "Hello Carol!\n";

    $p
        Less often used, the $(code:&dollar;map) syntax can also be used with subcodes that need multiple parameters by sharing common parameters:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: A(greet, name)
                $print $(greet), $(name)!

            page: t
                module: perl
                $map A(Hello), Alice, Bob, Carol

    $p
        Here $(code:Hello) is passed as the first parameter to every call, and the remaining parameters are mapped over one at a time.
    $p
        One of the benefits of $(code:&dollar;map) call is to allow certain use of macros:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            macros:
                Guests: Alice, Bob, Carol

            subcode: A(greet, name)
                $print $(greet), $(name)!

            page: t
                module: perl
                $map A(Hello), $(Guests)
    $p
        The macro is expanded before mapping, so both of the last two examples produce the same statements:
        &call codeprint, perl
            print "Hello, Alice!\n";
            print "Hello, Bob!\n";
            print "Hello, Carol!\n";
    $p
        It should be noted that the preprocessing directive $(a:$(code:&dollar;(for:...)),#preproc_for)&#44; introduced later in this chapter&#44; achieves a similar result.

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, nest_call, Nest call
    $p
        A similar but even more exotic syntax is the $(code:&dollar;nest) call. It works with a block subcode and nests the block calls, one level per parameter:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: each(x)
                $foreach $$(x) in @$(x)s
                    BLOCK

            page: t
                module: perl
                my @sizes = ("small", "large")
                my @colors = ("red", "blue")
                $nest each, size, color
                    $print $size $color
    $p
        $(code:&dollar;nest) is the syntax sugar for:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            &call each, size
                &call each, color
                    $print $size $color
    $p
        The output $(code:t.pl) would be:
        &call codeprint, perl
            &num;!/usr/bin/perl
            use strict;

            my @sizes = ("small", "large");
            my @colors = ("red", "blue");
            foreach my $size (@sizes) {
                foreach my $color (@colors) {
                    print "$size $color\n";
                }
            }
    $p
        Running it prints every combination:
        &call codeprint
            small red
            small blue
            large red
            large blue
    $p
        $(code:&dollar;nest) is most useful when the nesting is deep, or when the list of levels comes from a macro: if a macro $(code:dims) holds the list of levels, $(code:&dollar;nest each) can simply take $(code:&dollar;(dims)) as its parameters.


subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, milt_subcode, Multiply defined subcode
    $p
        A common usage pattern is to use $(code:subcode) as place holders, for example
        &call codeprint, mydef
            page: t
                $call @main_init
                $call main_loop
                $call finish
    $p
        $(code:main_init) is a placeholder that will have initialization code related to many different features. The $(code:@) prepended to the name suppresses warnings even when the subcode is not defined, perfect for placeholders.
    $p
        Now it is not desirable to mix code related to different semantic groups together even though they all need to happen at the same runtime context. For that, $(code:MyDef) allows a subcode to be multiply defined.
        &call codeprint, mydef
            &num; ---- feature_A.def ----

            subcode:: main_init
                feature A related initialization

            subcode: other_subcode_related_to_feature_A
                ...

        &call codeprint, mydef
            &num; ---- feature_B.def ----

            subcode:: main_init
                feature B related initialization

            subcode: other_subcode_related_to_feature_B
                ...

    $p
        Pay attention to the double colons after $(code:subcode). If double colons are omitted, only the first $(code:subcode:) definition will get loaded. This is where include files loading order matters. Unintentional subcode name collision should be avoided.
    $p
        Finally in the main $(code:.def) file, we can simply include the files:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            include: feature_A.def
            &num; include: feature_B.def  &num; selectively disable feature B

            page: t
                $call @main_init
                ...

    $p
        To see the effect, here is the same pattern in a single file:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode:: main_init
                feature A initialization

            subcode:: main_init
                feature B initialization

            page: t
                module: general
                $call @main_init
                main loop
    $p
        Both definitions are concatenated, so the output $(code:t.txt) is:
        &call codeprint
            feature A initialization
            feature B initialization
            main loop
    $p
        If the double colons are replaced with single colons, only the first definition is kept and $(code:mydef_page) warns about the second one:
        &call codeprint
            Not overwriting subcode main_init: [t.def - 1] -> [t.def - 4]
    $p
        and the output becomes:
        &call codeprint
            feature A initialization
            main loop

    $p
        It should be mentioned that in addition to $(code:subcode::), signifying concatenation, there are also $(code:subcode:@) and $(code:subcode:-). Both advise $(code:MyDef) on what to do when $(code:subcode) names collide. $(code:subcode:@) signifies lower priority. This is used to supply a default subcode definition in a library, and any colliding subcode definition will overwrite it. $(code:subcode:-) is similar to $(code:subcode::) and signals that the code should be concatenated. Unlike the double colon, $(code:subcode:-) always prepends the code to whichever definition was loaded earlier. It goes without saying that the effect of these special syntaxes depends on the loading order of the $(code:MyDef) parser and can be difficult to follow. In general, their use should be discouraged.

#---------------------------------------- 
subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, syntax_autoload, $(code:subcode: _autoload)
    $p
        There is a special subcode called $(code:_autoload). If defined, it will be automatically called before loading the $(code:page)'s main code.
    $p
        The function of $(code:_autoload) is similar to the multiply defined subcode above except that it is called automatically. For its role, $(code:subcode: _autoload) can be defined in multiple places and the code will be concatenated together even when the $(code:subcode:) line does not use double colons.

#---------------------------------------- 
subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, syntax_dump_stub, $(code:DUMP_STUB)
    $p
        A very similar effect as multiply defined subcode is $(code:DUMP_STUB). It is used like this:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: t
            DUMP_STUB main_init
            $call feature_A
            $call feature_B

        subcode: feature_A
            $(block:main_init)
                code initialization related to feature A
            feature A implementation

        subcode: feature_B
            $(block:main_init)
                code initialization related to feature B
            feature B implementation

    $p
        The output would be:
        &call codeprint
            code initialization related to feature A
            code initialization related to feature B
            feature A implementation
            feature B implementation
    $p
        $(code:DUMP_STUB) is a keyword for placeholders that will be replaced at the end with a named block of code. The syntax $(code:&dollar;(block:...)) places the code into that named block. The initialization code from both features was gathered into the $(code:main_init) block and inserted at the $(code:DUMP_STUB) position, before the feature implementations.
    $p
        $(code:&dollar;(block:...)) integrates with other relevant code more tightly and shares the same subcode scope. Sometimes this is more readable than the alternative of having a separate subcode.

#---------------------------------------- 
subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, macro_param, Inline macros with parameters
    $p
        It is also possible to define and use inline macros with parameters. For example:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $(set:m=$1[$2*$(N)+$3])
            $for i=0:$(N)
                $for j=0:$(N)
                    $for k=0:$(N)
                        $(m:C,i,j) += $(m:A,i,k)*$(m:B,k,j)
    $p
        With $(code:module: c) and $(code:N) set to 2&#44; this becomes:
        &call codeprint, c
            for (int i = 0; i<2; i++) {
                for (int j = 0; j<2; j++) {
                    for (int k = 0; k<2; k++) {
                        C[i*2+j] += A[i*2+k] * B[k*2+j];
                    }
                }
            }
    $p
        Inline macro parameters are not explicitly declared; they are simply marked by $(code:$1-9) and replaced at expansion time with a comma-separated list of values.
    $p
        Only single digit following $ is significant. For example:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            macros:
                A: $11

            subcode: test
                This is $(A:x) &num; x1

#---------------------------------------- 
subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, preproc_if, Preprocessing: $(code:&dollar;(if:...))
    $p
        Basic switching logic is supported with preprocessing directives $(code:&dollar;(if:...)), $(code:&dollar;(elif:...)), and $(code:&dollar;(else)). Each takes the indented block below it; the block ends with the indentation, so there is no closing directive:
        &call codeprint
            $(if:!A)
                Code when macro A is not defined
            $(elif:A=0)
                Code when A equals "0" (string comparison)
            $(elif:A>10)
                Code when A is numerically > 10
            $(else)
                Code for the remaining cases
    $p
        Here is a reference of all supported condition forms:
    $table ref
        $tr
            $th "Condition"
            $th "True when"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A))
            $td "macro A is defined and non-empty"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:!A))
            $td "macro A is not defined or empty"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A=val))
            $td
                macro A equals "val" (string comparison)
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A!=val))
            $td
                macro A does not equal "val"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A>10))
            $td "macro A is numerically greater than 10"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A>=10))
            $td "macro A is numerically greater than or equal to 10"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A&lt;10))
            $td "macro A is numerically less than 10"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A&lt;=10))
            $td "macro A is numerically less than or equal to 10"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A~pat))
            $td "macro A matches regex pattern (head match)"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A!~pat))
            $td "macro A does not match regex pattern"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A or B))
            $td "either A or B is true"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A and B))
            $td "both A and B are true"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:0)) / $(code:&dollar;(if:1))
            $td "always false / always true (for quickly disabling/enabling code)"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:hascode:name))
            $td
                subcode "name" is defined
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:A in x,y,z))
            $td "macro A equals one of the listed values"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:string:A))
            $td "macro A looks like a string literal"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:number:A))
            $td "macro A looks like a number"
        $tr
            $td
                $(code:&dollar;(if:word:A))
            $td "macro A looks like a simple word"
    $p
        A few notes on the conditions:
    $ul
        $li
            The left side of comparison operators ($(code:=), $(code:&lt;), $(code:&gt;), etc.) is always treated as a macro name. The right side is a normal expression. For example, in $(code:&dollar;(if:A>&dollar;(B))), $(code:A) is a macro name while $(code:&dollar;(B)) is expanded.
        $li
            $(code:or) and $(code:and) can combine conditions, but they are not implemented as full expressions. Nested combinations of $(code:!), $(code:or), and $(code:and) may not work as expected.
        $li
            $(code:&dollar;(if:0)) and $(code:&dollar;(if:1)) are special cases -- useful for quickly disabling or enabling a block of code.
    $p
        For the complete up-to-date implementation, see $(code:macros_compile/preproc.def), $(code:fncode: testcondition).

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, preproc_for, Preprocessing: $(code:&dollar;(for:...))
    $p
        The $(code:&dollar;for...) syntax provides quick way to multiplex code:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $(for:a in Alice, Bob, Carol)
                Hi, $(a)

            $(set:namelist=Alice,Bob,Carol)
            $(for:a in $(namelist))
                $(if:a=Alice)
                    Hi $(a), I love you!
                $(else)
                    Hi $(a)!
    $p
        produces:
        &call codeprint
            Hi, Alice
            Hi, Bob
            Hi, Carol

            Hi Alice, I love you!
            Hi Bob!
            Hi Carol!

    $p
        There is a shortcut syntax for specifying a simple range-based list:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            &num; reference: mydef_utils.def: fncode: get_range($a, $b)
            $(for:a in 1-11) &num; 1,2,3,...,11
                $print $(a)

            $(for:a in a-z) &num; a,b,c,...,z
                $print $(a)

    $p
        Sometimes we may need to multiplex over multiple lists:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $(for:a, i in x,y,z and 0,1,2)
                $(a) = $(i)
        &call codeprint
            x = 0
            y = 1
            z = 2

    $p
        There is also a syntax to use $(code:&dollar;(for:...)) anonymously:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $(for:Alice, Bob, Carol)
                Hi $1
        &call codeprint
            Hi Alice
            Hi Bob
            Hi Carol
    $p
        $(code:$1) is used to refer to the implicit macro for each case.
    $p
        Anonymous multiplexing over multiple lists also works:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $(for:x,y,z and i,j,k)
                A_$1 = $2;
        &call codeprint
            A_x = i;
            A_y = j;
            A_z = k;
    $p
        Up to nine lists are allowed, but you probably want to limit to a much smaller number.

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, preproc_set, Preprocessing: $(code:&dollar;(set:...))
    $p
        We already learned that $(code:&dollar;(set:...)) can be used to set local macros within the scope. What if we want to set macros to the parent scope? We can use $(code:&dollar;(set-1&#58;...)):
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $call set_macros
            $print A = $(A)

            subcode: set_macros
                $(set-1:A=3.14)
    $p
        prints $(code:A = 3.14). Direct $(code:&dollar;(set:...)) won't work here as it will expire upon exit the subcode.
    $p
        We also can set macros at global level using $(code:&dollar;(setmacro:...)):
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: section(title)
                $(setmacro:id+=1)
                Section $(id). $(title)

            page: t
                $call section, Hello
                $call section, Bye
        &call codeprint
            Section 1. Hello
            Section 2. Bye
    $p
        We haven't introduced the $(code:+=) operator yet. Intuitively, $(code:+=) increments the macro definition numerically.
    $p
        $(code:+=) works for $(code:&dollar;(set:...)) and $(code:&dollar;(set-1&#58;...)) as well. However, it is difficult to track consistent macros across scopes, therefore, it makes most sense to only use these operators in $(code:&dollar;(setmacro:...)).
    $p
        In addition to $(code:+=), there are also $(code:-=), $(code:*=), and $(code:/=), which does their corresponding arithmetic updates. They are not as useful as $(code:+=) though.
    $p
        There is also a string concatenation operator $(code:.=), which appends the new definition to the old one.
    $p
        Very often when we need concatenate strings, we would like some separators into between the items. For that, we have another syntax:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: add_name(name)
                $(setmacro:namelist[,]=$(name))
                Hi $(name)!

            page: t
                $call add_name, Alice
                $call add_name, Bob
                We have gathered: $(namelist).
        &call codeprint
            Hi Alice!
            Hi Bob!
            We have gathered: Alice,Bob.
    $p
        Because the scoping rules, very rarely we need unset macros. When we need it, it is often only needed to shield certain macros from child scopes. For that, we have the $(code:&dollar;(unset:...)).
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $(unset:A,B,C)
            $call subcode_that_checks_macros

    $p
        Note that $(code:&dollar;(unset:...)) only unsets macros in the current scope, but will effectively shield macros defined in parent scopes. It only accepts a list of simple words.
    $p
        $(code:&dollar;(set:...)) sets one macro at a time, so it does not need any delimiters, and you can easily define macros with commas in them.
    $p
        Sometimes it is desirable to set multiple macros in a single line. For that, we can use $(code:&dollar;(mset:...)):
        &call codeprint, mydef
            $(mset:A=x,B=y,C=z)
    $p
        You can only set macros in the current scope with $(code:&dollar;(mset:...)), and you cannot have commas in the definitions.

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, preproc_scope, Preprocessing: scope rules
    $p
        Both $(code:&dollar;(for:...)) and $(code:&dollar;(if:...)) contains nested blocks. It should be noted that $(code:&dollar;(for:...)) will create additional child scopes, but $(code:&dollar;(if:...)) (and $(code:elif/else)) will not.
    $p
        The latter is useful as in the following case:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            subcode: greet(type, name)
                $(if:type=hot)
                    $(set:greeting=It is such an honor to meet)
                    $(set:ending=!!!)
                $(else)
                    $(set:greeting=Hello,)
                    $(set:ending=.)
                $(greeting) $(name)$(ending)

            page: t
                module: general
                $call greet, hot, Alice
                $call greet, cold, Bob
    $p
        The macros set inside $(code:&dollar;(if:...)) are still in scope after it:
        &call codeprint
            It is such an honor to meet Alice!!!
            Hello, Bob.

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, macro_special, Special macro syntax
    $p
        $(code:MyDef) implements a small set of special macro syntax for convenience:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            Type name 10 times: $(x10:NAME ) &num; NAME NAME ... NAME 
            Type name 10 times: $(x10, :NAME) &num; NAME, NAME,  ... NAME
            List of digits: $(join:pat $1:, :1-3) &num; pat 1, pat 2, pat 3
            Duplex list: $(join:$1_$2:, :x-z and 1-3) &num; x_1, y_2, z_3
            Multiplex list: $(join:$1_$2:, :x-z mul 1-3) &num; x_1, x_2, ..., z_3

            $(set:A=word)
            Perl one-liners: $(eval:ucfirst("$(A)")) &num; Word
    $p
        $(code:MyDef) is written in Perl, so it is trivial to add $(code:eval) and expose the entire Perl engine. There is also a similar interface for $(code:eval) subcode, which essentially allows direct perl code to manipulate text transformations. However, it is necessary to understand $(code:MyDef)'s internal details to effectively work with the rest of $(code:MyDef) processing. The $(a:output_general,#general) chapter goes into those details.
    $p
        For more up-to-date details, please reference the source.

#----------------------------------------
subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, syntax_clash, Collisions with target-language syntax
    $p
        $(code:MyDef) adds very little syntax of its own, but it processes the text before the target language ever sees it. A few $(code:MyDef) constructs -- comments, macros, and parameter lists -- can therefore collide with ordinary text in the output language. When the output looks wrong, check the generated file and watch for warnings such as $(code:Macro ... not defined) from $(code:mydef_page).

    $h4
        Comments: $(code:&num;) and $(code:/*)
    $p
        A line starting with $(code:&num;) is a $(code:MyDef) comment and is dropped. C preprocessor lines ($(code:&num;include), $(code:&num;define), $(code:&num;if), $(code:&num;ifdef), $(code:&num;else), $(code:&num;endif), $(code:&num;pragma) etc.) are recognized and kept. Other lines -- a shebang, or a comment in Perl, Python, or shell -- are removed.
    $p
        A $(code:&num;) with whitespace on both sides starts a trailing comment and the rest of the line is removed, even inside a string. A $(code:&num;) that is not surrounded by whitespace is safe:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            page: t
                module: general
                x = "a &num; b"
                y = "a&num;b"
    $p
        produces:
        &call codeprint
            x = "a
            y = "a&num;b"
    $p
        Similarly, a line starting with $(code:/*) begins a multi-line comment that is removed through the closing $(code:*/).
    $p
        Workarounds:
    $ul
        $li
            Start the line with a hex escape. A leading $(code:&#92;xNN) is decoded when the output is written, so $(code:&#92;x23!/bin/sh) outputs $(code:&num;!/bin/sh) and $(code:&#92;x2f* ... */) outputs a C comment. Only a leading escape is decoded.
        $li
            Define a macro for the character, e.g. $(code:H: &num;) under $(code:macros:), and write $(code:"a &dollar;(H) b"). Macros are expanded after comments are filtered.
        $li
            Use the target language's own escape, e.g. $(code:"a &#92;x23 b") in a Python or Perl string.

    $h4
        Macros: $(code:&dollar;&#40;) and $(code:&dollar;.)
    $p
        $(code:&dollar;&#40;) followed by a word character is a macro reference. An undefined macro expands to nothing, with a warning. This collides with shell command substitution $(code:&dollar;(date)), Makefile variables $(code:&dollar;(CC)), and jQuery $(code:&dollar;(document)). In addition, $(code:&dollar;.) is a shorthand for the macro $(code:&dollar;(this)), which collides with Perl's $(code:&dollar;.) and jQuery's $(code:&dollar;.ajax).
    $p
        Forms that do not start with a word character are safe: $(code:&dollar;( date )), $(code:&dollar;((1+2))), $(code:&dollar;("&num;id")), as are ordinary variables such as $(code:&dollar;x) and $(code:&dollar;{x}).
    $p
        Workarounds:
    $ul
        $li
            Start the line with $(code:&dollar;: ) -- the rest of the line is output literally without macro expansion. (Comment filtering still applies.)
        $li
            Define a macro for the character, e.g. $(code:D: &dollar;), and write $(code:&dollar;(D)(date)).
        $li
            Start the line with $(code:&#92;x24), e.g. $(code:&#92;x24(CC) -o prog).
    &call codeprint, mydef
        macros:
            D: $

        page: t
            module: general
            d=$(date)            &num; macro "date" -- expands to nothing
            d=$(D)(date)         &num; d=$(date)
            $: CMD = $(CC) -c    &num; CMD = $(CC) -c

    $h4
        Commas in parameters
    $p
        Parameters of $(code:&dollar;(name:...)), $(code:&dollar;call), and $(code:&dollar;map) are separated by commas. If a macro uses fewer parameters than given, the extra ones are silently dropped:
        &call codeprint, mydef
            macros:
                code: <code>$1</code>

            page: t
                module: general
                $(code:a, b)         &num; <code>a</code> -- ", b" is lost
                $(code:f(a, b))      &num; <code>f(a, b)</code>
    $p
        Commas inside parentheses, brackets, or quotes are protected, so $(code:f(a&#44; b)), $(code:[1&#44; 2]), and $(code:"1&#44; 2") each stay a single parameter. A macro holding a comma does not help, because it is expanded before the parameters are split. Otherwise, rephrase or use an escape of the target language, such as $(code:&amp;&num;44;) in HTML.

    $h4
        Page attributes and keywords
    $p
        Lines of the form $(code:word: value) at the very start of a page are read as page attributes (such as $(code:module:) and $(code:output_dir:)) and do not appear in the output. If the output itself starts with such a line (YAML, for instance), put $(code:&dollar;: ) in front of it.
    $p
        A few words are $(code:MyDef) keywords when they appear alone on a line: $(code:BLOCK), $(code:NEWLINE), $(code:NOOP), and $(code:DUMP_STUB name). Lines starting with $(code:&dollar;call), $(code:&call), $(code:&dollar;map), and similar are always $(code:MyDef) calls. Output modules add their own keywords (e.g. $(code:&dollar;if) and $(code:&dollar;print)).

#----------------------------------------
subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, syntax_fncode, $(code:fncode:) -- function definitions
    $p
        While $(code:subcode) is expanded inline at compile time, many languages have their own concept of functions that are called at runtime. $(code:MyDef) supports this with $(code:fncode:):
        &call codeprint, mydef
            page: t
                module: perl
                $print "3 squared is %d", square(3)

            fncode: square($x)
                return $x * $x
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_run t.def
            PAGE: t
              --> [./t.pl]
            perl ./t.pl
            3 squared is 9
    $p
        and $(code:t.pl) is:
        &call codeprint, perl
            &num;!/usr/bin/perl
            use strict;

            printf "3 squared is %d\n", square(3);

            &num; ---- subroutines --------------------------------------------
            sub square {
                my ($x) = @_;
                return $x * $x;
            }
    $p
        $(code:fncode:) is defined at the top indentation level, just like $(code:subcode:). The key differences are:
    $ul
        $li
            $(code:fncode:) generates a function in the target language (e.g. $(code:sub) in Perl, a C function in C).
        $li
            $(code:fncode:) is called using the target language's normal function call syntax, not $(code:$call).
        $li
            Parameters are declared in the $(code:fncode:) line and automatically unpacked in the generated code.
    $p
        The exact behavior of $(code:fncode:) depends on the output module. For example, $(code:output_perl) generates $(code:sub) definitions with automatic parameter unpacking from $(code:@_), and only includes functions that are actually used in $(code:.pl) scripts. See the output module chapters for details&#44; starting with $(a:output_perl,#perl) and $(a:output_c,#c).

#----------------------------------------
subcode:: sections
    $call section, invoke, Invoking $(code:MyDef)

    $call subsection, invoke_config, $(code:config) file
    $p
        The suite of $(code:MyDef) tools will always check for a $(code:config) file (a text file named $(code:config)) in the same directory where the main $(code:.def) file is located.
    $p
        Strictly speaking, a $(code:config) file is not always necessary. $(code:MyDef) options can be set in three places: inside the $(code:.def) source, on the command line, and in the $(code:config) file. The $(code:config) file is a convenient means to share default settings across an entire project.
    $p
        When the $(code:config) file is missing and we invoke $(code:mydef_make) to create a $(code:Makefile), it will always create a $(code:config) file, so further invocations of $(code:mydef_make) will not prompt you with questions again and again.
    $p
        Basic $(code:config) file only contains two settings. Following is the one for the $(code:MyDef) repository:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        output_dir: MyDef
        module: perl
    $p
        $(code:output_dir) specifies the directory where the output files should be put. If this option is missing, the default is to put output files in the same directory of the $(code:.def) sources. For big projects, some separation is desirable.
    $p
        $(code:module) specifies the output module to use. When this option is missing, the default is to use $(code:output_general). $(code:MyDef) is written in Perl, so for its own repository the module is $(code:output_perl).
    $p
        Another often used options is $(code:include_path), which is a '$(code::)' separated list of directories for searching for $(code:include:) files. The option if provided gets prepended to environment variable $(code:MYDEFLIB). The current folder will always get searched first.
    $p
        The $(code:output_general) module does not need many options, but many language- and application-specific output modules do need additional options to control behavior. The $(code:config) file can contain arbitrary options, and it is the output module's responsibility to check these options.
    $p
        Internally, $(code:MyDef) does not really differentiate between macros and options. Both are simply name-value pairs. Everything specified in $(code:MyDef) is internally available in a global reference hash variable $(code:$MyDef::var).

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, mydef_page, $(code:mydef_page)
    $p
        $(code:mydef_page) is the $(code:MyDef) compiler. It reads in a $(code:.def) (and all the included $(code:.def) files included within), translates it, and writes to output files, one per $(code:page:) directive.
    &call codeprint, sh
        $ mydef_page -m[module] -o[outputdir] src.def
    $p
        $(code:-m) specifies output module to use. $(code:-o) specifies output directory that output files will go. There should not be spaces between the option and value.
    $p
        Both options can be omitted. If the module is missing, it takes from $(code:config) file. If $(code:config) does not exist or the module option is not set within, it will check for module option inside the source $(code:.def) file. If still not found, $(code:mydef_page) will complain and exit.
    $p
        However, when the module option is given via command line or $(code:config) file, that module will be used. And when the module option under $(code:page:) directive mismatches this module, the corresponding page will be ignored. For example:
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_page -mgeneral t.def
            skipped 1 pages (due to module mismatch), use -m to override default module.
    $p
        It is possible to have multiple pages defined in a single $(code:.def) file. All pages that match the module option will be compiled in a single invocation. If multiple pages require different output modules, multiple invocations of $(code:mydef_page) with different module options are necessary to compile every page.

    $p
        $(code:-o) specifies the output location. If not specified, it will search similarly in the $(code:config) file. If not found, it defaults to the current working directory.
    $p
        If the source $(code:.def) file also specifies $(code:output_dir) under the $(code:page:) directive, the behavior depends on whether an absolute or relative path is given.
    $p
        If an absolute path is given, it will be used as the output location.
    $p
        If a relative path is given instead, it will be appended to the path given on the command line or $(code:config) file, or the default $(code:.) (current directory).
    $p
        When everything goes well, it looks like the following:
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_page t.def
            PAGE: t
              --> [./t.pl]
    $p
        If multiple pages are being compiled, each of them will be listed.
    $p
        If $(code:MyDef) encounters errors, there will be error messages:
        &call codeprint t.def
            $ mydef_page t.def
            PAGE: t
            [t.def:4] Code A not found!
            [t.def:5] Macro B not defined
            --> [./t.pl]
    $p
        The output file will still be produced despite the errors. It is important to pay attention to error messages.
    $p
        File locations are usually reported along with errors. However, it should be noted that the current implementation of file location tracking is imprecise and can often be off by a few lines. Nevertheless, you should be able to locate the error with the information given in the error message. This will certainly get fixed at some point. However, since it does not affect the actual compilation, only the error reporting, it is currently not a high priority.

    $p
        Although it is possible, it is not recommended to directly output scripts (such as Perl/Python scripts) into the installation destination. It is prudent to always test locally and run the extra install steps when satisfied. The exception is generating web pages. Web pages need to be viewed through a browser anyway, so it is convenient to have $(code:output_dir) directly point to your web root.

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, mydef_make, $(code:mydef_make)
    $p
        When projects grow to contain more than one output file, a $(code:Makefile) is helpful to make the edit-compile-debug cycle easier. We believe in keeping things simple, and believe a simplified, hand-edited Makefile is better than using typical build tools. Build tools generate $(code:Makefile)s without surfacing the decisions. In the effort to accommodate the most complicated projects, they produce the most complicated solutions even for the simplest projects. That is the opposite of "keeping it simple".
    $p
        Nevertheless, hand-generating a $(code:Makefile) can be tedious. $(code:mydef_make) can be used to generate the initial $(code:Makefile) by scanning all $(code:.def) files within the directory as well as all subdirectories. It will generate all the rules from $(code:def) files to output files using $(code:mydef_page) commands. For simple projects, the $(code:Makefile) from $(code:mydef_make) will be good enough. Both the $(code:MyDef) project and its output module projects are examples where $(code:mydef_make) is good enough.
    $p
        When it is not good enough, you are encouraged to hand-edit your $(code:Makefile). If you insist on using build tools, you can always opt to have $(code:def) source files outside of the build tree and have $(code:mydef_page) output into the build tree. That way, your build tool can work as if you are directly editing the code rather than generating it from $(code:def) files. There may even appear to be no difference to your colleagues. One could edit the $(code:Makefile) to have make automatically invoke the build tool upon updates.
    $p
        If you run $(code:mydef_make) the first time (when $(code:config) file does not exist):
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_make
            Please enter the path to compile into [out]:

            Please enter module type [perl]:

              output_dir: out
            Create output folder out ...
    $p
        $(code:mydef_make) prompts you for output_dir and module type. It creates a $(code:config) file and a $(code:Makefile). If the $(code:output_dir) does not exist, it will create it for you.
    $p
        If you run it again, it will only update $(code:Makefile):
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_make
                output_dir: out

subcode:: sections
    $call subsection, mydef_run, $(code:mydef_run)
    $p
        At the opposite end of building big projects, you may be coding a single program that consists of a single file (not necessarily a single $(code:def) file). In this case, invoking build tools or even generating a $(code:Makefile) appears to be overkill. All we would like is a single keypress from source to result.
    $p
        $(code:mydef_run) is specifically for this purpose. It can be invoked like this:
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_run t.def
            PAGE: t
              --> [out/t.pl]
            perl out/t.pl
            Hello  World!
    $p
        Basically it compiles from $(code:def) to output, then based on a set of heuristics about the output file type, runs the appropriate compiler or interpreter to execute the program.
    $p
        Here is an example with C:
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_run t.def
            PAGE: t
              --> [out/t.c]
            gcc -std=c99 -O2 -oout/t out/t.c  && out/t
            Hello World!
    $p
        Here is an example with Java:
        &call codeprint, t.def
            $ mydef_run t.def
            PAGE: t
              --> [out/t.java]
            javac out/t.java && cd out && java t
            Hello World!
    $p
        Of course not all types of file have a way to run it:
        &call codeprint, sh
            $ mydef_run t.def
            PAGE: t
              --> [./t.txt]
            do not know how to run it
    $p
        Well, at least it is honest and it compiles for you.
    $p
        If you configure the command to a hotkey in your editor, then the edit-debug cycle is shortened into a single keypress. If you use $(code:vim), put this in $(code:.vimrc):
        &call codeprint, vim
            :nmap <F5> :!mydef_run %<CR>
    $p
        For details of the heuristics or customization, refer to $(code:mydef_run.def) in the source repository.
    $p
        It is possible to directly set the command for $(code:mydef_run) to run with $(code:cmd&#58;). For example:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: t
            module: general
            cmd: cat t.txt

            Hello World!

    &call codeprint, sh
        $ mydef_run t.def
        PAGE: t
          --> [./t.txt]
        cat t.txt
        Hello World!
    $p
        $(code:run&#58;) instead puts a prefix in front of the usual command: with $(code:run&#58; time)&#44; a Perl page runs as $(code:time perl ./t.pl).

    $p
        Or you may simply specify an argument:
    &call codeprint, mydef
        page: t
            module: perl
            arg: P1 P2 P3

            $foreach $a in @ARGV
                $print argument $a

    &call codeprint, sh
        $ mydef_run t.def
        PAGE: t
          --> [./t.pl]
        perl ./t.pl P1 P2 P3
        argument P1
        argument P2
        argument P3
    $p
        As a bonus, do you know that you can directly use $(code:mydef_run) on $(code:.pl), $(code:.c), or any source code with a known extension? Try it.

